WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:05.860
Secure C sharp applications start with maintainable foundations.

2
00:00:05.960 --> 00:00:13.640
Use supported .NET versions, maintained frameworks, and dependency versions that your team can patch.

3
00:00:13.740 --> 00:00:20.540
An application built on unsupported runtime assumptions can become difficult to secure even if its business logic is well written.

4
00:00:20.820 --> 00:00:23.380
Configuration should be separate from code.

5
00:00:23.480 --> 00:00:37.800
Connection strings, API endpoints, feature flags, identity provider settings, certificate references, and deployment-specific values should be managed through appropriate configuration and deployment systems.

6
00:00:37.900 --> 00:00:45.420
Source code should not become the place where production secrets, one-off environment changes, or temporary operational decisions live forever.

7
00:00:45.700 --> 00:00:49.460
Reducing unnecessary surface area is a security habit.

8
00:00:49.560 --> 00:01:02.120
Remove endpoints that are not needed, packages that are no longer used, permissions that are broader than the application requires, background services that do not support the product, and debug behaviors that belong only in development.

9
00:01:02.220 --> 00:01:06.300
Small systems are easier to understand and easier to review.

10
00:01:06.580 --> 00:01:19.700
.NET and ASP.NET Core include security features for authentication, authorization, antiforgery protection, data protection, logging, configuration, dependency injection, and secure transport settings.

11
00:01:19.800 --> 00:01:25.640
These features are most useful when teams understand why they are enabled and what assumptions they depend on.

12
00:01:25.740 --> 00:01:31.100
A framework default is a starting point, not a substitute for design review.

13
00:01:31.380 --> 00:01:34.820
Templates and sample code should be treated as scaffolding.

14
00:01:34.920 --> 00:01:43.240
They help teams move quickly, but they may include placeholder behavior, development settings, broad examples, or simplified flows.

15
00:01:43.340 --> 00:01:53.500
Before production use, review templates, defaults, middleware order, error settings, logging settings, identity configuration, and deployment assumptions.
